Sculpt and rig the **Siege Husk**, a decrepit, low-poly shambling humanoid enemy for
the Siege first-person voxel last-stand game — as **one continuous skin** in a 24x48x20
volume using only the `mc-skin` tool. You build it by **compositing signed-distance
primitives** into a single whole-body field — one recorded operation at a time — bind it
to a **skeleton you invent**, and **author its animations** as F-curves so the skin
deforms across its joints.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief tells
you *what the husk is* and *how it must move*, but the forms you sculpt, the skeleton you
build, where you place the bones and joints, and how the skin binds to them are
**entirely yours to invent**. Work out the bones a walking, lunging, collapsing humanoid
needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to sculpt the field and edit the skeleton is the `mc-skin` binary already
on your `PATH`. You build the character by calling it **one operation at a time**, and
the ordered operations you issue — recorded to `actions.json` — together with the
skeleton and animations you define are your submission.

`mc-skin` sculpts **one whole-body field** — the entire husk at once. There is **no
`--part` flag**: a skinned character is a single field with a single log, extracted into
one continuous Marching Cubes surface (a low-poly, chunky faceted look — the binary's
fixed character). Composite the body from additive and subtractive spheres, boxes,
ellipsoids, and cylinders — each an opaque `#rrggbb` `--color`, with an optional soft
`--blend` that fuses them into one skin. The volume starts **empty**. Each call only
records the operation and renders **nothing** — run `mc-skin render` to (re)draw
`model.png`, and run `mc-skin render --time <ms> --animation <name>` to see the model
**posed with actual skin deformation**; you **must** run `mc-skin render` before you
finish so it emits the skinned `mesh.glb` geometry and the derived skin weights the run
is scored from — an unrendered model is scored as empty.

**You invent the skeleton.** Define the bones you need with `define-bone` (the first bone
the root, each other under a parent), position each head→tail with `set-bone`, place
joints on them with `define-joint`, and author each required animation with
`define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. The per-vertex skin weights are **derived
automatically** at render (bone-heat diffusion, capped at four influences per vertex) —
you do not paint them by hand.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, for example:

```
mc-skin add-ellipsoid --x 12 --y 30 --z 10 --rx 4 --ry 7 --rz 3 --color "#7e8a68"
mc-skin define-bone --name pelvis --parent ""
```

(The values above are just an example — sculpt your own forms and name your own bones
however you like.) Run `mc-skin --help` to list every operation **and** the skeleton and
animation subcommands (defining and positioning bones, defining joints, and authoring
animations with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`), and `mc-skin <operation> --help` for
one operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. **Read
`model.png` after you run `mc-skin render`** to see the whole body, and read a posed
`mc-skin render --time <ms> --animation <name>` to confirm the skin actually deforms
across its joints.

